# Markdown Formatting Rules

## Purpose

- MUST follow these common Markdown rules when creating or editing Markdown documents in an editor or IDE.
- MUST prioritize readability and consistency.

## Heading Hierarchy

- MUST use `#` only once, as the document title at the top of the file.
- MUST use `##` for section headings.
- MUST use `###` for subsections and `####` for smaller nested headings.
- MUST keep heading levels in order without skipping levels, such as `##` to `###` to `####`.
- NEVER jump from `##` directly to `####`.
- MUST NOT use heading levels deeper than `####`.
- SHOULD follow this structure:

```markdown
# Document Title

## Section Heading

Content...

### Subsection Heading

Content...

#### Small Heading

Content...
```

## Code Blocks

- MUST use fenced code blocks for multi-line code.
- MUST include a language identifier on fenced code blocks.
- SHOULD use an accurate language identifier such as `javascript`, `python`, `csharp`, `bash`, `text`, or `markdown`.
- MUST use inline code formatting for short code, commands, file paths, environment variables, identifiers, and literal values.
- NEVER use four-space indentation to create code blocks.
- MUST distinguish inline code and block code correctly.
- SHOULD write examples like a fenced JavaScript block with `javascript` as the language identifier.

## Links and Images

- SHOULD use inline links, for example `[Link text](https://example.com "Optional title")`.
- SHOULD use a title attribute when it improves accessibility.
- MAY use reference-style links when that improves readability.
- MUST make link text descriptive.
- SHOULD avoid direct raw URL display at the start of lines because it reduces readability.
- MUST keep direct URL paste to the minimum necessary.
- MUST write images with Markdown image syntax and meaningful alt text when images are used.
- SHOULD write image links with the same clarity as normal links, for example `![Alt text](image-url "Optional title")`.

## Quality Checklist

- MUST verify that H1 to H4 heading order is not skipped.
- MUST verify that code blocks are fenced and have language identifiers.
- MUST verify that inline code and block code are used in the correct places.
- MUST verify that link text is descriptive and URLs are correct.
- MUST verify that direct URL display is minimized.
